<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" ?>
<!--
  DYX 3WD — Fast DDS (rmw_fastrtps_cpp, ROS 2 Humble = Fast DDS 2.6) default profiles.

  Installed ONCE to /etc/dyx3/fastdds_profiles.xml (an upgrade never overwrites an existing copy, like the *.env files). The launchers
  export it as FASTRTPS_DEFAULT_PROFILES_FILE when it exists (deployment/scripts/dyx3-env.sh).

  Deliberately NOT in this file: reliability kind, history, durability and depth. The nodes declare the per-topic QoS in code and that
  stays the single owner. RMW_FASTRTPS_USE_QOS_FROM_XML is NOT set, so the code QoS is never replaced from here.

  1. Participant: UDPv4 only, shared memory off.
     DERIVED — NOT FROM V1 SPEC: architecture section 8 says the prototype's fastdds_no_shm.xml existed for a reason to re-derive
     and neither inherit nor drop blindly. The reason recorded here is deterministic recovery after a process crash: Fast DDS
     shared-memory segments and port mutexes under /dev/shm can outlive a crashed participant and block or delay rediscovery, which
     matters because any node exiting restarts the whole graph (control_graph.launch.py). Every node is on this host's loopback, so UDP
     adds no network hop, and the loopback UDP cost is negligible at this graph's rates. To be confirmed by the bench measurement M2
     in docs/reviews/2026-10-10_production_blockers_transport_timing_review.md; if M2 shows a cost or no recovery benefit, change it
     there, not by habit.
     The transport is whitelisted to 127.0.0.1 so this file can never widen what ROS_LOCALHOST_ONLY=1 restricts (see the descriptor).

  2. DataWriter: reliable max_blocking_time 10 ms.
     DERIVED — NOT FROM V1 SPEC: 10 ms is one dyx3_px4_link writer period (publish_rate_hz 100 -> 10 ms). A reliable KEEP_LAST(1) writer
     whose reader stops acknowledging blocks publish() for up to max_blocking_time (Fast DDS default 100 ms), and px4_link publishes
     five messages per cycle on its single executor thread, so one stuck reader could otherwise cost many cycles. Only the blocking
     bound is set; the kind stays whatever the code declares.
-->
<profiles xmlns="http://www.eprosima.com/XMLSchemas/fastRTPS_Profiles">

  <transport_descriptors>
    <transport_descriptor>
      <transport_id>dyx3_udp_v4</transport_id>
      <type>UDPv4</type>
      <!-- Loopback only, like ROS_LOCALHOST_ONLY=1. The rmw adds its own 127.0.0.1-whitelisted UDPv4 transport for that
           variable but keeps the user transports of the default participant profile: an unrestricted transport here would
           reopen discovery on eth0 and the hotspot. A bench that needs a second machine on the graph edits this list too. -->
      <interfaceWhiteList>
        <address>127.0.0.1</address>
      </interfaceWhiteList>
    </transport_descriptor>
  </transport_descriptors>

  <participant profile_name="dyx3_default_participant" is_default_profile="true">
    <rtps>
      <useBuiltinTransports>false</useBuiltinTransports>
      <userTransports>
        <transport_id>dyx3_udp_v4</transport_id>
      </userTransports>
    </rtps>
  </participant>

  <data_writer profile_name="dyx3_default_datawriter" is_default_profile="true">
    <qos>
      <reliability>
        <max_blocking_time>
          <sec>0</sec>
          <nanosec>10000000</nanosec>
        </max_blocking_time>
      </reliability>
    </qos>
  </data_writer>

</profiles>
